I started by recognizing that the load balancer needed to match incoming request hostnames against a set of routing rules to determine which backend to use. I used regex patterns stored in a dictionary to define flexible matching rules for different hostname patterns - some exact matches like 'www.domain.org' and some wildcard patterns like '.*.example.com'. When a request comes in, the function iterates through each pool's regex pattern and returns the corresponding backend once it finds a match, using the pool's index position to map it to the appropriate backend server. I chose to index into the backends list by finding the pool name's position in the pools dictionary to keep the routing logic simple and maintainable. If no patterns match the hostname, the function returns None to indicate no suitable backend was found, which allows the caller to handle the unmatched case however they need.

import re

pools = {
    'pool1': re.compile('.*.example.com'),
    'pool2': re.compile('www.domain.org')
}

backends = ['www.backend1.com', 'www.backend2.com']

def get_backend(request):
    host = request.hostname

    for pool_name, pattern in pools.items():
        if pattern.match(host):
            idx = list(pools.keys()).index(pool_name)
            return backends[idx]

    return None

